    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3). The acceptance package is the only test module and it's green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional, city-region-validated `postcode` support for owner creation, keeping the request contract backward-compatible.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema (`type: string`, `pattern: ^[0-9]{4}$`). Because `Owner` is `allOf` `OwnerFields`, this gives both the input DTO (`OwnerFieldsDto`) and output DTO (`OwnerDto`) the field, so it's accepted, stored, and returned. The pattern also provides Bean-Validation format enforcement.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `postcode` field (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it by name in both directions, so no mapper change was needed.\n\n3. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added a `postcode` column to the `owners` table (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the schema).\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Added `isPostcodeValid(owner)` returning `400` when invalid. The rule:\n   - Absent/empty \u2192 valid (optional).\n   - Present \u2192 must be exactly 4 digits; and when the city maps to a known region (reusing `ownerMapper.locality`, the existing fixed table), the value must fall in that region's inclusive range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099). A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode.\n\nThe three acceptance tests confirm: a valid postcode is stored/returned (`2000`), an owner without a postcode is still accepted, and a malformed postcode (`12`) is rejected with 400.",
